oying a special meal, not ecstatic celebration.\n\n---\n\n**3) Evidence of catching vs receiving the crab?**\n\n**Answer: Strong evidence that the crab was received (purchased or gifted), not caught.**\n\n**Evidence:**\n- **No fishing/harvesting activity** is shown. No crab pots, boats, ice holes, or nets.\n- The crab is **already cooked and portioned** when first seen (frames 1–8), indicating it was prepared off-camera by someone else.\n- The man is holding it on a **serving tray with a small container of butter**, suggesting it was served to him.\n- The transition from **outdoor public space → hotel room → dining table** supports the idea of acquiring the meal externally and bringing it back to eat.\n- In frames 12–18, the man and boy are **eating and cracking legs**, not preparing or cooking them — further evidence the crab was pre-cooked and ready to eat upon arrival.\n\n---\n\n**4) Presence of locals/guides in outdoor scenes?**\n\n**Answer: Yes, at least one other person is visible, possibly a local or guide.**\n\n**Evidence:**\n- In **frame 3**, a second person in a blue jacket and helmet is visible in the background, standing near a snowmobile. This person is not part of the core family (man and boy), and their attire and position suggest they may be a guide, fellow traveler, or local.\n- In **frame 4**, another man (wearing a “SMITH” beanie and camouflage neck gaiter) is seen looking at the crab tray — he is not the main man (who is in a black jacket in frame 1), suggesting he is a separate individual, possibly a local or guide.\n- These individuals are **not seen indoors** with the family, implying they are external to the family unit and may have been involved in providing the crab.\n\n---\n\n**5) State of crab (raw/alive vs cooked/ready-to-eat)?**\n\n**Answer: The crab is cooked and ready-to-eat.**\n\n**Evidence:**\n- In **all frames where the crab is visible (1–24)**, it is **bright red-orange**, the color of cooked crab. Raw king crab is typically brown